{"judgments": [
{"clause_id": "c051", "issues": [
  {"issue": "\"Company Expenses\" is defined by a broad list closing with \"any extraordinary expenses of the Company\", an open-ended catch-all against enumerated specifics. Its outer reach against the carve-out for Loan Services expenses is contestable.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.28},
  {"issue": "The exclusion of Loan Services expenses, which \"shall be borne by the Manager or the Servicer\", must be squared with 5.3(f), which caps Servicer compensation and refers reimbursable expenses back to this paragraph. The circular cross-reference invites argument.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.24}
]},
{"clause_id": "c052", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The paragraph defines \"Permitted Expenses\" to include \"the foregoing expenses and indemnifications\", although no indemnification is mentioned in this paragraph and the defined term is never used again in the Agreement. The definition is left without work to do.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.26},
  {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
]},
{"clause_id": "c053", "issues": [
  {"issue": "Indemnification is extended to \"the members, officers, employees, and consultants of the Company\", but the proviso excluding willful misconduct or gross negligence is framed by reference to \"the Manager's duties under the operating agreement\". The conduct standard does not match the class of indemnitees.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.36},
  {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.15}
]},
{"clause_id": "c054", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The catch-all \"or similar compensation for services\" following an enumerated list of construction, brokerage, property management and development fees leaves the sweep of the Manager's retained compensation uncertain. The list-plus-catch-all structure is exactly the ejusdem generis problem.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.30},
  {"issue": "Letting the Manager keep fees \"received ... with respect to an entity in which the Company has a debt or equity interest\" is difficult to square with 5.4, which purports to state the amounts and times at which the Company pays the Manager. Whether 5.4 is an exhaustive compensation scheme is arguable.", "type": 2, "prob": 0.27}
]},
{"clause_id": "c055", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The Loan Origination Fee is \"in an amount determined on a case-by-case basis\", which fixes no limit at all on affiliate compensation. Read with 5.6, which makes such fees the Manager's property, the absence of any cap is a live construction point.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.31},
  {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
]},
{"clause_id": "c056", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The condition requires \"commercially reasonable efforts to sell any property ... at a fair price on the open market for at least ninety (90) days\", with neither \"commercially reasonable efforts\" nor \"fair price\" defined. Both are standards a buyer and a lender would apply differently.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.37},
  {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.13}
]},
{"clause_id": "c057", "issues": [
  {"issue": "The price constraints can be mutually unsatisfiable: the price must be \"no higher than the independently appraised value\" yet \"no lower than the total amount of the Company's 'investment'\", which may exceed appraised value in a foreclosure. The provision gives no rule for that case.", "type": 1, "prob": 0.44},
  {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.16}
]},
{"clause_id": "c058", "issues": [
  {"issue": null, "type": 1, "prob": 0.13},
  {"issue": null, "type": 2, "prob": 0.14}
